---

## WordPress automation — read before touching a live site

Getting any of these wrong costs hours, and none of them are obvious from the
error message.

**Never send a browser User-Agent.** Hosting firewalls block outdated browser
strings on sight, because that is what malicious traffic pretends to be. Send a
descriptive one naming the software and where to complain about it:

```
[AGENCY]-ContentSync/1.0 (WordPress REST client; +https://clientsite.com)
```

If being blocked ever tempts you to disguise the client better, that instinct
is the bug. Identify it properly instead.

**Stop when the success rate collapses.** Aggressive retries are what firewalls
greylist. Run one job at a time, back off in minutes rather than milliseconds,
and if requests start failing consistently, stop — continuing renews the block
rather than getting through it.

**Read the value back before believing a write failed.** Page builders cache
rendered output independently of the host cache, so a saved change can look
exactly like a failed save. Clear the builder cache first, then the host.

**Back up every record before writing to it.** One JSON file per post, kept in
the repo, turns any mistake into a one-line revert.

**Look at the rendered page when the job is done.** Automated checks confirm
data, not design. Content can be present, correct, resolving — and invisible to
a reader.

**Grep for `TODO FIXME XXX TBD PLACEHOLDER LOREM WIP` before publishing.**
Placeholders that were harmless in drafts go public the moment you publish.

**Close wp-admin tabs before running a job.** An edit screen opened before an
API write, then saved, submits the stale form it loaded with and silently
overwrites everything.

Fuller detail, including host-specific behaviour and status codes:
`docs/wordpress-automation-playbook.md`

